{
  "requirement": "Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST NOT offer a waiver for an uncited first-party Rust function. When a function serves only as an internal implementation detail and no existing requirement explains it, Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST direct agents to propose an explicit project requirement scoped to one area of internal code, such as `internal.config_parsing`, rather than one catch-all requirement, obtain the user's agreement, mark the function with `{impl}`, and run `habeas author --all` for the harness to assess that requirement's evidence. That assessment MUST account for whether the requirement explains each implementing function's behavior. An internal-detail requirement MUST NOT justify an otherwise unspecified end-user-observable behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met by the three bundled skills, and a test covers it.\n\n**Implementation.** `md/requirements/cli.md` anchors the requirement with `:impl:` on all three bundled skills. These are the only skills in `assets/skills`. Each skill covers every obligation:\n- **No waiver.** `habeas-requirements` says \"there is no uncited-function waiver\". `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-evidence` say \"There is no waiver for an uncited function\". A ripgrep for waive, exempt and uncited in `assets` found no passage offering a waiver.\n- **Scoped requirement.** Each skill tells agents to propose an explicit requirement \"scoped to that area of internal code, such as `internal.config_parsing`, rather than one catch-all\".\n- **Agreement, `{impl}` and authoring.** Each skill tells agents to get the user's agreement, mark the function with `{impl}`, and run `habeas author --all` \"so the harness assesses that requirement's evidence\".\n- **Assessment accounts for each function.** Each skill says the review \"checks that the requirement explains each implementing function\".\n- **No end-user-observable behavior.** Each skill says: \"Do not use an internal-detail requirement to justify otherwise unspecified end-user-observable behavior.\"\n\nI found nothing in the skills beyond what the requirement and its sibling requirements (`requirements_review_skill`, `behavior_review_guidance`) specify. The `{cite}` in `md/reference.md` is a legitimate context reference; the text there is documentation, not the implementation.\n\n**Tests.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs::bundled_skills_direct_scoped_internal_requirements_instead_of_waivers` carries `{test}` for the requirement.\n- It runs against all three skill files, embedded with `include_str!`, and normalizes whitespace so phrases can span wrapped lines.\n- It asserts the no-waiver statement and every guidance phrase listed above.\n- It asserts that none of \"may waive\", \"can waive\", \"waiver applies\" or \"is exempt\" appears, which covers the \"MUST NOT offer a waiver\" side.\n\nThese are string-match tests, which is the practical way to check static guidance text. The phrase list matches each clause of the requirement.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.internal_detail_guidance",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.internal_detail_guidance"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} internal_detail_guidance",
        "Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST NOT offer a waiver for an uncited first-party Rust function. When a function serves only as an internal implementation detail and no existing requirement explains it, Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST direct agents to propose an explicit project requirement scoped to one area of internal code, such as `internal.config_parsing`, rather than one catch-all requirement, obtain the user's agreement, mark the function with `{impl}`, and run `habeas author --all` for the harness to assess that requirement's evidence. That assessment MUST account for whether the requirement explains each implementing function's behavior. An internal-detail requirement MUST NOT justify an otherwise unspecified end-user-observable behavior.",
        ":::"
      ]
